// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LESSONS — exactly 4 per module (32 total). Every lesson's content weaves in
// a learner-friendly explanation, why the process matters, required checks,
// required evidence, permitted action, approval boundary, prohibited action,
// escalation condition, and a known limitation where relevant; the exact
// source citation is carried separately in each lesson's `source` field.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

export const LESSONS = [
  // ---- Module 1 — Customer Service Foundation and Governance ----
  lesson('cs-m1-l1', 'cs-m1', 1, 'Purpose, Three Interaction Goals and Non-Negotiables',
    'The handbook frames Customer Support as a business-protection function, not simply message-answering. Every customer interaction has three goals, in priority order: protect the marketplace account, resolve the customer\'s issue correctly, and protect the company financially. This matters because an eBay or Amazon account defect costs the business far more than any single refund, and threatens the whole team\'s revenue channel, not just one order. Before any of the three goals can be treated as satisfied, the order must be verified rather than assumed (Module 2 covers verification in full) — there is no evidence requirement at this foundational stage beyond that. Agents may resolve queries using the handbook\'s own documented rules and templates; nothing here authorises stepping outside documented authority. It is never acceptable to reply without checking the data first, to promise something that has not been internally confirmed, to share personal or warehouse contact details, or to tell a customer to "open a case." Escalate whenever a documented rule does not clearly cover the exact situation, rather than improvising a resolution. A known limitation of this lesson is that it introduces the three goals in principle only — the specific numeric thresholds and category-by-category actions that make them concrete are taught from Module 2 onward.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Foreword and Chapter 7 ("The Three Goals of Every Customer Interaction"; "The Marketplace Protection Rule")'),
  lesson('cs-m1-l2', 'cs-m1', 2, 'Roles, Responsibility Boundaries and Generic Approval Hierarchy',
    'The handbook defines a small hierarchy of roles; this programme always uses generic role titles rather than any real employee name. A Marketplace Agent (or Delivery Support Agent, depending on channel) handles standard queries within documented rules. The Team Head is the first escalation point for anything above the agent\'s own authority. The Operations Manager is the final operational authority for higher-value or exception decisions. Management handles safety recalls, legal threats and strategic decisions. The Accounts/Admin Role handles invoices, VAT and payment-gateway matters, which sit outside an agent\'s own scope entirely. This matters because knowing exactly who owns a decision prevents both an agent acting outside their authority and a decision being needlessly delayed at the wrong level. Before escalating, identify which role the decision actually belongs to; the required evidence for any escalation is a case summary, what has been verified, what has already been told to the customer, and the specific decision needed. An agent may act independently only within their own documented threshold (Lesson 4); anything above it needs the Team Head, then the Operations Manager, in that order, as value or risk increases. Never make a decision that belongs to a higher role, and never treat a verbal instruction alone as sufficient — it must be documented. Escalate whenever a case falls outside the agent\'s own documented authority. A known limitation is that the source names specific individuals for these roles; this programme deliberately uses only the generic role titles above.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 8 ("Customer Support Team Structure — The Escalation Hierarchy") and Chapter 50 ("Internal Escalation Framework")'),
  lesson('cs-m1-l3', 'cs-m1', 3, 'Rule Hierarchy and the Six Pre-Reply Gates',
    'When rules could conflict, the handbook sets a strict five-level order: Safety rules always win first; then Marketplace rules; then Financial Risk rules (the approval thresholds); then Standard Operating rules; and only last, a narrow Agent Discretion that never overrides any level above it. Separately, before any reply is sent, six pre-reply gates must be worked through in sequence: the Evidence Gate, the Verification Gate, the Safety Gate, the Approval Gate, the Policy Gate, and the Marketplace Gate. This matters because skipping a gate is exactly how expensive mistakes happen in practice — a discount offered before a safety check, or a reply sent before the order has even been verified. Work through all six gates before composing any reply, on every channel, every time; each gate has its own specific evidence requirement, checked in the module that covers that topic in detail. Proceed to the next gate only once the current one has passed. The Approval Gate specifically requires the action to sit within the agent\'s own authority; anything above it needs Team Head or Operations Manager approval first, from Lesson 2. Never skip a gate because a case "seems obvious," and never let Agent Discretion override a Safety, Marketplace, or Financial Risk rule. If the Policy Gate finds no documented rule for the exact scenario, escalate rather than improvise. There is no further limitation beyond what later modules add gate by gate.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 11 ("Master Rule Architecture — Rule Hierarchy; Global Rules") and Chapter 12 ("Decision Tree & Gate Logic System — The Universal Pre-Reply Gates")'),
  lesson('cs-m1-l4', 'cs-m1', 4, 'BLOS Approval Thresholds, No-Match Protocol and Governance',
    'BLOS (Business Logic and Operations Settings) is the handbook\'s name for the numeric thresholds that govern financial decisions. The core thresholds every agent must know: an agent may act up to £50 without approval; £50 to £100 requires Team Head approval; above £100 requires Operations Manager approval; any return worth £100 or more also triggers a separate high-value handling protocol; and the maximum discount any agent or Team Head may offer is 35%, with anything above that requiring Operations Manager approval. This matters because these thresholds are the mechanism that prevents both under-refunding and over-refunding, and only the Operations Manager may change them. Always check a decision\'s value against these thresholds before acting; the required evidence for any threshold-crossing decision is a documented request stating the specific figure and reason. An agent may act independently only within the £50 band described above. It is never acceptable to offer a discount or refund above your own threshold without the correct approval already in place, and completing this training programme never authorises a learner to actually approve or process a live financial action. Where no documented rule matches a scenario at all, the No-Match Protocol applies: send a holding message, escalate to Team Head in full, and the decision is fed back afterwards so a new rule can be written. A known limitation is that this lesson states the thresholds exactly as the handbook defines them, without granting the learner any live approval authority.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 15 ("BLOS Governance System — Current BLOS Thresholds") and Chapter 14 ("Exception & No-Match Handling — The No-Match Protocol")'),

  // ---- Module 2 — Communication and Message Handling ----
  lesson('cs-m2-l1', 'cs-m2', 1, 'The 11 Message Categories and Priority Classification',
    'The handbook defines exactly 11 message categories, numbered 01 to 11: Return Query, Delivery Query, Pre-Sales Query, Admin/Account Issue (the mandatory default catch-all), Order Change Before Shipping, Defective Item, Damaged Item, Wrong Item Sent, Missing Parts/Wrong Quantity, Wrong Description, and Item Not Received. Classification always uses the customer\'s first message only; if a message mixes triggers from more than one category, the highest-risk element decides which one applies. Every message also receives a priority: HIGH by issue type (for example cancellation, safety, or an A-to-Z claim), HIGH by tone (urgent or distressed language), MEDIUM for standard operational queries, or LOW for general and pre-sales queries. This matters because the category decides which decision flow and which template apply, so getting it wrong produces the wrong resolution downstream. The required check before classifying is reading the full message thread, not only the latest message. There is no evidence requirement at the classification step beyond the message text itself. The permitted action is to assign exactly one category and one priority level per case. It is never acceptable to change a category based on a later follow-up message, or to leave a message uncategorised — where nothing else clearly fits, Category 04 is the mandatory default, never a blank. Escalation does not apply to classification on its own. Message category and message priority are two separate systems from each other, and both are separate again from a case\'s status (open, pending response, resolved, or escalated — Module 7) and from a marketplace\'s own account-health state (Module 6): none of these four systems should ever be used interchangeably. The handbook also runs a fifth, entirely internal system — a colour-coded quality check on the handbook\'s own content, used only by handbook governance to find gaps in the written rules — which is never a case status, a message category, or a priority level, and plays no part in how an agent classifies or prioritises a customer message. A known limitation is that the exact category wording is repeated in a later canonical register, covered in Module 8.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 16 ("Complete Message Categorisation System") and Chapter 54 ("Message Category Register — CS-061")'),
  lesson('cs-m2-l2', 'cs-m2', 2, 'Intent, Fraud Awareness and Seven-Step Order Verification',
    'Reading a message correctly means identifying what the customer actually wants, not only their literal wording, and reading the entire thread first — a previous message may already contain a promise that has to be honoured or corrected. Order verification is a mandatory seven-step sequence completed before writing any reply: confirm the Order ID, check dispatch status, check tracking, verify the delivery address, check the customer\'s return history, check other open orders, and check any marketplace notes. Fraud awareness means noticing — never accusing — specific signals such as three or more returns from the same customer in six months, a delivery claim that contradicts tracking evidence, or a request to move communication off-platform. This matters because replying without verification risks stating something false to the customer and creating a follow-up problem or dispute that verification would have prevented. All seven verification steps are required, every time, before any reply is composed; the verification results themselves become part of the case record as the required evidence. Once verification is complete, the agent may proceed to compose a reply. It is never acceptable to accuse a customer of fraud directly, or to take any financial action while fraud signals are still under review. Escalate to Team Head before any financial action once fraud signals are noticed — Module 6 covers the full fraud-signal register.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 17 ("Message Reading & Conversation Analysis") and Chapter 18 ("Order Verification Process")'),
  lesson('cs-m2-l3', 'cs-m2', 3, 'Response-Building Framework and Evidence-Before-Reply',
    'A correct decision still needs a correctly written reply. The handbook\'s anatomy of a correct response is: a personalised greeting using the customer\'s name; an acknowledgement of their message before any solution; an apology only where the fault is genuinely Ledsone\'s own; a clear, specific action statement, never a vague phrase such as "we will look into it"; a specific evidence request where one is needed; clear next steps with a stated timeframe; and a named closing signature. No offer, promise, or resolution may be given before the relevant evidence for that issue type has been received and verified — evidence always comes before action, never the reverse. This matters because a vague or premature reply creates confusion, unnecessary chase messages, or a promise the business cannot actually keep. Work through each element of the response anatomy before sending; the required evidence is whatever the specific issue type requires, covered fully in Modules 3 through 5 and 7. A reply may be sent once the anatomy and the evidence gate are both satisfied. Any threshold-crossing content inside the reply still needs the Module 1 approvals obtained first. It is never acceptable to send a resolution, offer, or promise before evidence is verified. If evidence cannot be obtained, follow the specific evidence-failure handling covered in Module 7. Exact wording standards for tone are covered next, in Lesson 4.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 19 ("Response Building Framework — The Anatomy of a Correct Response")'),
  lesson('cs-m2-l4', 'cs-m2', 4, 'Tone, Approved Phrasing, Prohibited Phrasing and Multilingual Boundaries',
    'Tone must stay professional and warm regardless of the customer\'s own tone — a rude message never earns a cold or defensive reply. The handbook requires exact substitutions for specific situations: never say "open a case" — say "we are checking this with our marketplace on your behalf"; never reveal an internal discount limit — say "we are checking with our team and will come back to you with our best offer"; never share personal contact details — direct the customer back to the official platform messaging system instead. Certain phrases are explicitly prohibited in any customer-facing reply, including "I completely understand your frustration," "calm down," "as I said in my previous message," "there is nothing I can do," an unpaired "that\'s not our policy," "you should have...," and opening a reply with "unfortunately." For non-English markets, English is the primary standard; any German-language reply uses the formal "Sie" form and only an approved translated template. This matters because the exact wording protects the marketplace relationship and prevents an accidental admission or an unkeepable promise. Compare a drafted reply against the approved and prohibited phrasing lists before sending; approved phrasing may be personalised to the specific case. It is never acceptable to use any of the prohibited phrases above, or to compose a non-English reply without fluency or an approved template — escalate instead. A known limitation is that this lesson repeats only the source\'s own explicitly required or prohibited phrases; it does not add general communication advice from outside the handbook.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 20 ("Marketplace Communication Rules — Approved Phrasing Reference"), Chapter 21 ("Language to Avoid") and Chapter 22 ("Multilingual & International Support Standards")'),

  // ---- Module 3 — Delivery and Courier Management ----
  lesson('cs-m3-l1', 'cs-m3', 1, 'Delivery-Query Classification and Verification',
    'Delivery queries are the highest-volume category the team handles, so recognising the specific scenario correctly matters more here than almost anywhere else. Before replying to any delivery query, the seven-step order verification from Module 2 must already be complete, so the agent knows the tracking status, the last scan, the estimated delivery date, and the delivery address before writing a single word. The handbook describes a range of distinct delivery scenarios, from a parcel moving normally, to no tracking movement yet, to a failed delivery attempt, to a parcel returning to sender, to a delivery that is genuinely delayed and needs investigation. This matters because each scenario has its own correct handling, and misreading "no scan yet" as "lost" — or the reverse — leads to the wrong action and either an unnecessary refund or an under-served customer. The required checks are tracking status, last scan, address match, and whether an exception code is present; the required evidence is the tracking record itself. Where tracking is moving normally, the permitted action is to reassure and inform; where it is not, the permitted action is to investigate internally before replying. Approval boundaries and prohibitions here are inherited from the Module 1 gates; it is never acceptable to tell a customer a parcel was not dispatched before confirming the reason internally first. Escalation for genuinely delayed or lost parcels is covered in the next two lessons.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 23 ("Delivery Query Handling — The 12 Delivery Scenarios")'),
  lesson('cs-m3-l2', 'cs-m3', 2, 'Courier Evidence, Tracking Checks and Customer Updates',
    'Ledsone uses several courier services, each with its own tracking system and investigation process, and each requires reading its tracking data correctly before contacting the customer. When a parcel shows no scan yet, the correct first step is checking with the postage team internally — never telling the customer it "was not sent." For genuinely delayed parcels, the handbook requires contacting the courier to open an investigation and giving the customer a specific, honest update with a follow-up timeframe, rather than a vague "please wait." A returned-to-sender parcel has several distinct causes — no one home, wrong address, refused delivery, a customs rejection, a cancellation, or transit damage — and each cause has its own resolution rather than one universal response. This matters because an update built on an unverified guess creates a false promise, and a missed courier-investigation step can turn a simple delay into a loss the company cannot recover. The required checks are the courier tracking history and any exception code, confirmed internally with the postage team before any customer-facing claim about dispatch status; the required evidence for a courier escalation is the full tracking history. The permitted action is a holding message with a specific timeframe while investigating. It is never acceptable to state a parcel\'s dispatch or delivery status without checking it in that exact moment. Escalate a courier investigation once the internal check confirms a genuine delay, not before — the next lesson covers the most sensitive delivery scenario, INR, in full.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 23 ("Delivery Query Handling — Scenarios D5-D8"), Chapter 25 ("Courier Management System") and Chapter 26 ("Courier Escalation & Appeals")'),
  lesson('cs-m3-l3', 'cs-m3', 3, 'INR Investigation and Courier Escalation Process',
    'An Item Not Received (INR) claim is one of the most sensitive scenarios in the handbook, because issuing a refund without proper verification both loses money and fails to identify what actually went wrong. The handbook sets a six-gate verification sequence before any INR resolution is offered: tracking status, delivery proof, timeline, address match, any courier exception code, and whether a marketplace case is already open with its own deadline. Where tracking shows "delivered" with a photo at the correct address but the customer still says they did not receive it, the correct response is never an automatic refund — it is to ask the customer to check a safe place or a neighbour, raise a courier investigation, and escalate to Team Head before any refund is considered. This matters because a genuine loss deserves a fast, fair resolution, but an unverified refund on a parcel tracking shows as delivered is a direct financial loss with no way to recover it afterwards. All six INR gates are required, in order; the required evidence is the tracking history, any delivery photo, and a courier investigation reference once one is opened. Once the courier confirms the parcel is genuinely lost, the permitted action is offering the customer a choice between replacement and full refund, with the replacement value following the Module 1 thresholds. It is never acceptable to auto-refund an INR claim before the gates are worked through; escalate to Team Head before any refund whenever delivery is disputed against clear tracking evidence. A known limitation: the handbook\'s own figures for exactly how many days must pass before a parcel is treated as lost are disputed between internal sources and are deliberately not taught in this programme — this lesson teaches the verification and escalation workflow only, using the courier\'s own investigation outcome, not a day count, as the trigger for declaring a parcel lost.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 24 ("INR (Item Not Received) Handling System — INR Verification Gate System; INR Resolution Decision Logic")'),
  lesson('cs-m3-l4', 'cs-m3', 4, 'Delivery Exceptions, Holding Responses and Closure Evidence',
    'Some delivery situations fall outside the routine scenarios: an international customs hold, a customer who refuses delivery at the door, and cases where a courier investigation is still open while a marketplace deadline is approaching. For customs issues, the handbook does not authorise automatically paying or reimbursing customs fees — that decision escalates to Team Head. For a refused delivery, the customer\'s reason decides the resolution: a change of mind follows the standard return policy, while a refusal because of visible box damage follows the damage-handling process instead. Where a courier investigation has not concluded but a marketplace deadline is close, the marketplace-first rule applies: protect the account by acting on the deadline, then continue the courier claim separately afterward. This matters because treating every delivery exception the same way, or letting a courier investigation silently run past a marketplace deadline, creates an avoidable account-level problem. The required checks are the specific reason behind the exception and whether any marketplace deadline is at risk; the required evidence for a formal courier claim is the full pack — tracking history, delivery photo if any, order details, proof of dispatch, customer communication, and the marketplace case reference. The permitted action is a holding message while an exception is investigated. Customs-fee decisions and international high-value return-method decisions escalate to Team Head; it is never acceptable to assume a customs fee is automatically the company\'s responsibility. Escalate whenever a marketplace deadline is approaching and the courier outcome is still unknown. A known limitation carries over from the previous lesson: the disputed lost-parcel timing figures are not used here either.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 23 ("Delivery Query Handling — Scenarios D10-D11"), Chapter 26 ("Courier Escalation & Appeals — Evidence Required; Protecting Marketplace Accounts During Courier Investigations")'),

  // ---- Module 4 — Customer Returns, Refunds and Warranty ----
  lesson('cs-m4-l1', 'cs-m4', 1, 'Return Eligibility and Claim-Window Calculation',
    'Every return is either buyer-side (the customer\'s own reason — change of mind, no longer needed) or seller-side (Ledsone\'s error — wrong item, damage, defect, missing parts, or a listing mismatch), and this distinction decides who pays return shipping: the buyer for buyer-side returns, Ledsone for seller-side returns. The canonical rule for every claim window is that it always starts from the confirmed delivery date — the date courier tracking confirms the parcel reached the customer — never the order date, dispatch date, or invoice date. The specific windows are: 30 days for change-of-mind or doesn\'t-fit returns; 60 days for a defective or damaged-in-transit claim; no fixed window for a wrong item, missing parts, or a not-as-described claim, each raisable any time after delivery; and 3 years for a warranty claim covering a genuine manufacturing defect. This matters because using the wrong start date, or the wrong window for the wrong reason, can wrongly refuse a valid claim or wrongly accept an invalid one. The required check is confirming the exact "Delivered" scan in tracking before calculating any window; the required evidence is that confirmed delivery date. Once the delivery date and the return reason are both confirmed, the permitted action is applying the matching window; standard-window returns then follow the Module 1 refund thresholds. It is never acceptable to use the customer\'s stated receipt date over the tracking-confirmed date unless tracking is clearly wrong, or to guess a delivery date when no "Delivered" scan exists — escalate to Team Head instead.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 27 ("Return Policy Master System — The Two Types of Returns; Return Eligibility Matrix") and Chapter 51 ("Claim Window Definitions — Start Date, Duration & Rules, Rule CS-051")'),
  lesson('cs-m4-l2', 'cs-m4', 2, 'Refund/Replacement Decision Sequence and Evidence',
    'A refund is never the automatic first response to a complaint. The handbook sets a strict resolution priority order for confirmed seller-side issues: replacement or exchange first, because it resolves the customer\'s underlying need without a cash outflow; partial refund or discount second, for items still usable despite a minor issue; full refund with return third, once replacement is not possible; and full refund without return last, reserved for very low-value items or items unsafe to return. No resolution of any kind may be offered before the evidence required for that issue type has been received and verified. This matters because skipping straight to a refund both costs the company unnecessarily and can leave a customer without the product they actually wanted. The required check is confirming which resolution tier applies before making any offer; the required evidence is the specific set for that issue type, with the full matrix covered in Module 7. The permitted action is offering the first applicable resolution tier only, and once a return has actually been received, processing the refund within 48 hours of that receipt — not within 48 hours of when the customer first asked. Refunds and replacements above £50 need Team Head approval; above £100 need Operations Manager approval; a refund requested with no return received at all needs Team Head approval regardless of value. It is never acceptable to offer a full refund first when a replacement is available and the customer has not explicitly refused one, or to leave a confirmed refund unprocessed beyond the 48-hour window. Escalate any refund that crosses a threshold, or any refund requested without a return, before processing it.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 28 ("Refund Decision Engine — Resolution Priority Order; Refund Processing Rules") and Chapter 32 ("Refund Escalation Rules")'),
  lesson('cs-m4-l3', 'cs-m4', 3, 'Partial-Refund and Discount Boundaries',
    'A partial refund — a discount to let the customer keep the item — is only appropriate when the item is still safe, still functional, and the issue is cosmetic or a minor aesthetic difference, never for a safety-critical fault or a fully non-functional item. The handbook sets a damage discount matrix from 5% (a barely visible mark) up to a firm 35% maximum, and the escalation flow is: assess from photos, make the matching first offer, and only move toward a higher offer, a replacement, or a full refund if the customer declines. No agent or Team Head may offer above 35% without Operations Manager approval, and there is a hard safety override — any item with electrical damage, burn marks, exposed wiring, or cracked glass gets no discount at all, only a replacement or refund. Certain items should never be collected back at all — a single glass lampshade, a single non-metal LED driver, anything with burn marks or electrical safety damage, broken glass, or anything where return postage would cost more than the item is worth — for these, resolve directly without requesting a return. This matters because discounting an unsafe item, or collecting an item that cannot survive the return journey, both create unnecessary risk and cost. The required checks are photo evidence of the damage level and a safety check, in that order, before any percentage is selected; the required evidence is clear photos. The permitted action is offering the matching percentage from the matrix, escalating only as the customer declines. It is never acceptable to discount a safety-critical fault. Escalate to Operations Manager before offering above 35%.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 29 ("Partial Refund Rules — The Damage Discount Matrix; Discount Escalation Flow; Collection Exceptions")'),
  lesson('cs-m4-l4', 'cs-m4', 4, 'Labels, Warranty, Approval and Escalation',
    'Return labels come in several forms — QR code, printable PDF, courier collection, international, or the customer\'s own carrier — and every label must have its expiry checked before it is sent, since an expired label is a common, avoidable failure. Warranty claims follow a timeline: 0-30 days is handled as a standard return; 31-60 days is handled as a product-fault claim; from 61 days out to 3 years is a warranty claim, and no resolution may be promised to the customer before Team Head approval; beyond 3 years there is no warranty obligation at all, and any exception is a goodwill decision requiring Operations Manager approval. High-value returns of £100 or more trigger a separate protocol: report immediately, do not process anything until the case is acknowledged, monitor the return, and only refund or replace after the warehouse has inspected the returned item. This matters because an unchecked label, an unapproved warranty commitment, or a high-value item processed without inspection each create either a customer problem or a real financial exposure. The required checks are label expiry, the warranty-window position, and whether the £100-or-more protocol applies. The required evidence for a warranty claim is fault evidence plus proof of the confirmed delivery date; for a high-value return it is the warehouse\'s own inspection confirmation before any refund. The permitted action is issuing a matching label, or escalating a warranty claim beyond 60 days. It is never acceptable to commit to a warranty resolution before Team Head approval, or to refund a high-value return before warehouse inspection.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 27 ("High-Value Return Protocol"), Chapter 30 ("Return Label Management") and Chapter 31 ("Warranty Handling System")'),

  // ---- Module 5 — Product Issues and Technical Support ----
  lesson('cs-m5-l1', 'cs-m5', 1, 'Damaged and Defective Product Handling',
    'Damaged (transit-caused, external) and defective (an internal fault, no external damage) items follow related but distinct frameworks. For damage: request evidence first — outer packaging, inner packaging, and the item itself from multiple angles — before any offer; check for safety risk; assess whether the item still functions; classify the damage level against the Module 4 discount matrix; and evaluate whether the item should be collected at all. For a defective item: request evidence — a photo or video of the fault, the product label, the packaging; verify it matches Ledsone\'s own SKU and packaging; and route an invisible fault (recheck connections and compatibility first) differently from a visible defect (report to quality control and replace without delay, never with a discount). This matters because an offer made before photos are reviewed, or a discount offered on a genuinely non-functional item, both create cost and trust problems. The required checks are safety risk first, then functionality, in that order, before any resolution; the required evidence is the specific photo or video set for the issue type. The permitted action is replacing a confirmed visible defect without delay, or discounting only a confirmed cosmetic, safe, functional issue. It is never acceptable to offer any discount for a non-cosmetic defective fault, or to make an offer before evidence is reviewed. Escalate before replacing if every unit of a product is reported failing at once, since this usually signals an installation or compatibility issue rather than a batch defect.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 33 ("Damaged Item Handling — The 5-Step Damage Handling Framework") and Chapter 34 ("Faulty Product Troubleshooting — The Universal 3-Step Defective Flow")'),
  lesson('cs-m5-l2', 'cs-m5', 2, 'Wrong Item, Missing Parts and Wrong-Quantity Handling',
    'For a wrong item, the handbook is explicit about wording: never say "we sent the wrong item" — say "there appears to have been a mix-up with your order," and never reveal that an out-of-stock substitute was sent intentionally. Verification compares the order, the internal pick list, and what the customer actually received, all three, before replying. A discount to let the customer keep a wrong item is offered only when it is genuinely a functional alternative — same voltage, wattage, and fitting — never for an electrically incompatible item. For missing parts, the first step is verifying from photos, or internal packing records, before sending anything; small non-critical parts such as screws or clips can be sent immediately, while critical parts need careful verification first; and the same SKU being flagged repeatedly for missing parts is escalated to the packing team, not simply resolved case by case. This matters because the wrong wording can accidentally admit a specific internal error, and sending parts before verifying can mask a genuine packing-process problem that keeps recurring. The required checks are order versus pick list versus received item for a wrong item, and photo or internal-record confirmation for missing parts; the required evidence is as stated. The permitted action is replacing, discounting where genuinely functional, or sending missing parts once verified, following the Module 4 thresholds for any value involved. It is never acceptable to reveal an intentional out-of-stock substitution, or to send critical parts without verification. Escalate a repeated same-SKU missing-parts pattern to the packing team.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 35 ("Missing Parts & Wrong Item Handling — Missing Parts Golden Rules; Wrong Item Golden Rules")'),
  lesson('cs-m5-l3', 'cs-m5', 3, 'Product Technical-Support Boundaries and Evidence',
    'Technical questions — the wattage needed, an IP rating, dimmer compatibility — must always be answered from the verified product listing, never guessed; where the listing does not contain the answer, the agent escalates rather than estimating. The handbook gives specific, source-backed answers to common product questions across LED drivers, bulbs, lamp holders, cables and connectors, plugin pendants, and ceiling or wall fittings — for example, a cooling fan running on a driver is normal behaviour, not a fault; a higher-wattage driver than ordered is safe and beneficial, not a defect; a humming or burning smell from a driver is safety-critical and must be treated as such. This matters because an unverified technical answer can cause a real compatibility failure, a safety incident, or an unnecessary return. The required check is confirming the answer against the verified listing, or the specific source-backed product rule, before replying; the required evidence is the product listing itself, or the customer\'s stated wattage, voltage, or fitting details for a compatibility check. The permitted action is answering only what is verified, and providing the product manual for installation guidance rather than improvised electrical advice; CE or UKCA compliance-certificate requests always route to the Accounts/Admin Role. It is never acceptable to confirm a product is CE certified without the actual certificate reference, or to give installation guidance beyond what the manual states — this programme does not authorise the learner to act as a technical certifier. Escalate any answer the listing cannot confirm, and any compliance-certificate request.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 34 ("Faulty Product Troubleshooting — Product-Specific Troubleshooting Protocols") and Chapter 36 ("Product Information & Technical Support")'),
  lesson('cs-m5-l4', 'cs-m5', 4, 'Safety, Recall and Compliance Escalation',
    'A safety complaint overrides every other process in the handbook. The exact protocol is: tell the customer to stop using the item immediately, as the very first sentence of the reply; never offer a discount for a safety issue, under any circumstance; notify Team Head in the same session, not at the end of the day; request evidence — photos, a description of what happened, and any injury or property damage; offer a full replacement or a full refund, the customer\'s choice, with no extra conditions; issue a prepaid return label and document the case fully; and where an injury or property damage has occurred, escalate to management immediately, with no further commitments made without management guidance. This matters because a delayed or minimised response to a genuine safety issue risks real harm, which is why this scenario sits above every approval threshold covered elsewhere in this programme. The required check is whether any indicator of physical danger is present — an electrical fault, exposed wiring, burn marks, broken glass, or an injury report; the required evidence is photos and a description of what happened. The permitted action is a full replacement or a full refund only, the customer\'s choice; this protocol applies regardless of the item\'s value. It is never acceptable to offer a discount, to ask a customer to keep using an item to "test" it, or to make a compliance claim without the actual certificate. Escalate immediately, always, to Team Head, and to management as well if injury or property damage occurred — this programme never authorises the learner to make a legal, regulatory, or recall determination themselves.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 37 ("Safety, Compliance & Recall Handling — Safety Complaint Protocol; CE Certification and Compliance Queries")'),

  // ---- Module 6 — Marketplace Protection and Risk ----
  lesson('cs-m6-l1', 'cs-m6', 1, 'Marketplace Case and Account-Protection Principles',
    'eBay and Amazon set their own rules and measure seller performance continuously; how the team handles messages and cases directly shapes that measurement. The handbook\'s core protection principle is that when a customer opens a formal marketplace case, the first concern is preventing the marketplace from stepping in and deciding the case automatically, because that outcome creates an account-level problem that costs far more than the underlying refund would have. The specific, non-negotiable actions that protect this are: never tell a customer to "open a case" — use the approved marketplace-checking phrasing instead; respond to marketplace cases within the response window the handbook states; and, for eBay specifically, upload a return label to an open case before its deadline even where the customer has verbally agreed to a replacement instead. This matters because these are the actions the customer support team directly controls that most affect the account\'s standing. The required check is whether a marketplace case is open and what its response deadline is; the required evidence is the case reference and its stated deadline. The permitted action is responding within the case\'s window using approved phrasing. It is never acceptable to let a case deadline expire without action, or to use language that directs the customer to escalate further. Escalate immediately if a case cannot be resolved before its deadline. A known limitation: this programme deliberately does not restate specific numeric account-health metric thresholds (for example, percentage-based defect-rate scores) for any marketplace — those figures are owned and taught by the Amazon Team and eBay Team onboarding programmes; this module focuses on the case-response actions the Customer Service team is responsible for.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 7 ("Marketplace Protection Mindset — The Marketplace Protection Rule") and Chapter 38 ("eBay Account Health Protection — eBay Case Strategy")'),
  lesson('cs-m6-l2', 'cs-m6', 2, 'eBay and Amazon Case-Handling Responsibilities Supported by the Handbook',
    'The two marketplaces the handbook documents case-handling procedures for in real depth are eBay and Amazon; other channels are not covered to the same level of detail. For eBay, every open case is reviewed at the start of a working session, responded to within the source-stated window, and — critically — a prepaid return label is uploaded to the case itself, not just the message thread, before the deadline, regardless of any verbal agreement reached with the customer. For Amazon, an A-to-Z Guarantee Claim is treated as the single highest-priority task the moment it appears: gather all evidence — order details, tracking, full communication history, a delivery photo if available — and respond within the source-stated deadline; if the claim is genuinely valid, a proactive refund before Amazon decides avoids the account-level impact entirely. This matters because both marketplaces auto-decide against the seller if their own deadline passes, regardless of how justified the seller\'s position actually was. The required checks are the case or claim deadline and evidence completeness; the required evidence is full tracking, order details, and communication history for any A-to-Z response. The permitted action is filing the case response or label upload once evidence is assembled, with the underlying refund or replacement still following the Module 4 thresholds. It is never acceptable to let an eBay case close without seller resolution, or to miss an Amazon claim deadline. Escalate immediately if either deadline cannot be met with the current evidence. A known limitation: this programme does not teach detailed case-handling procedures for Wayfair, B&Q, or Avasam, because the handbook does not document those in the same depth as eBay and Amazon — any such case follows the no-match escalation rule from Module 1 instead.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 38 ("eBay Account Health Protection — eBay Case Strategy") and Chapter 39 ("Amazon Account Health Protection — A-to-Z Guarantee Claims")'),
  lesson('cs-m6-l3', 'cs-m6', 3, 'Negative Feedback, Fraud Signals and Abuse Escalation',
    'Negative feedback is addressed by resolving the underlying issue first, then privately and politely referencing that resolution and asking whether the customer would reconsider it — never offering an incentive to do so, and never arguing publicly. Fraud and abuse signals are specific, documented patterns, not general suspicion: three or more returns from the same customer in six months; a non-delivery claim that contradicts confirmed tracking evidence; a wrong-item claim that contradicts the internal pick list; a story that changes between messages; a refund demand paired with a threat of marketplace action; a request to move communication off-platform; and multiple identical orders each with a separate problem claim. The handbook\'s rule is that three or more of these signals in a single case trigger a mandatory stop: no further financial action, a holding message only, and a full escalation to Team Head. This matters because treating every claim as automatically genuine ignores a real cost risk, while accusing a genuine customer of fraud creates real legal and relationship risk — the correct response is always to document and escalate, never to accuse. The required check is counting the signals present in the specific case; the required evidence is a documented account of what was observed and when. Standard handling continues until three signals are reached. It is never acceptable to accuse a customer directly, or to process any refund, replacement, discount, or return label once three signals are present, until Team Head has decided. Escalate at the three-signal threshold, always, in full, to Team Head.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 40 ("Negative Feedback Management") and Chapter 53 ("Fraud Signal Register — CS-047, The 3-Signal Trigger")'),
  lesson('cs-m6-l4', 'cs-m6', 4, 'Crisis Handling, High-Risk Cases and Management Escalation',
    'A crisis is different from an individual case: it is a single event affecting many customers at once — a courier batch failure delaying many orders, a batch product defect across one SKU, or a marketplace policy change affecting current practice. The handbook\'s response pattern for each is coordinated, not case-by-case: identify every affected order, escalate to Team Head and, where the issue is systemic, to Operations Manager or management immediately, and prepare one proactive communication for all affected customers rather than waiting for each of them to contact separately. Individual refund or replacement decisions are held back until the scale and cause of the crisis are actually confirmed, so the company avoids making duplicate, unverified commitments. This matters because handling a systemic event as if it were many unrelated individual cases wastes time, risks inconsistent customer communication, and can commit the company financially before the true scale is known. The required check is whether this is a single isolated case or matches a crisis pattern — the same SKU, the same courier, the same policy change, affecting multiple customers; the required evidence is the list of affected orders and the confirmed cause. The permitted action is sending a proactive, consistent holding communication once a crisis pattern is confirmed. Individual financial decisions pause until Team Head or management confirm scope — this is a management-level decision, not an agent-level one. It is never acceptable to resolve individual crisis-linked cases ahead of the coordinated response, or to make a legal or regulatory determination about a batch defect. Escalate immediately, to Team Head and management, once a crisis pattern is suspected.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 42 ("Crisis Management Procedures — Crisis Types and Response Protocol")'),

  // ---- Module 7 — Evidence, Audit and Internal Operations ----
  lesson('cs-m7-l1', 'cs-m7', 1, 'Evidence Gate and Evidence Requirements by Issue',
    'The Evidence Gate is simple in principle and strict in practice: no refund, replacement, discount, or seller-side return label may be offered until the evidence required for that specific issue type has been received and verified — receiving a photo is not the same as having verified it matches the claim. The handbook\'s evidence matrix sets a specific list per issue type: a damaged item needs outer-packaging, inner-packaging, and multi-angle item photos, all three, not just one; a defective item needs a photo or video of the fault plus the product label; a wrong item needs a photo of the received item and its label or barcode; missing parts need a photo of everything received laid out together; item-not-received always needs tracking history and, where applicable, a delivery photo and a courier investigation reference, with no exceptions; and a warranty claim beyond 60 days always needs Team Head approval in addition to the fault evidence. This matters because skipping or half-completing an evidence check is exactly how a genuinely fraudulent claim gets paid, and how a genuinely valid claim gets wrongly delayed. The required checks are the full checklist for the specific issue type, every item, before proceeding; the required evidence is as listed per issue type above. The permitted action is proceeding to a resolution only once every required item is received and verified; certain evidence types, such as warranty claims beyond 60 days and formal courier claims, always require Team Head approval regardless of value. It is never acceptable to treat one unclear photo as sufficient evidence. Escalate where evidence cannot be obtained (Lesson 2 covers the fallback process) or where it contradicts the claim.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 43 ("Evidence Requirement Matrix") and Chapter 55 ("Evidence Requirement Matrix — CS-045, canonical version")'),
  lesson('cs-m7-l2', 'cs-m7', 2, 'Audit Trail, Case Status and Documentation',
    'The handbook\'s documentation rule is direct: if it is not recorded, it did not happen — an action taken but not documented cannot be verified, defended in a marketplace dispute, or reviewed for a pattern later. Every case record must capture the order ID and marketplace, the customer\'s original message, the assigned category and priority, the verification results, what evidence was received and what it showed, the decision made and why, any approval obtained and from whom, the action actually taken, the date and time of every outbound message, the case status — open, pending response, resolved, or escalated — any fraud or abuse signals noted, and the closure date and outcome. This matters because documentation is what turns "I did the right thing" into something that can actually be checked, and it protects both the customer and the agent if a case is disputed later. The required check is that every one of the listed fields is captured as the case progresses, not only at the end; the case record itself is the required evidence. A case may be closed only once every required field is complete. It is never acceptable to leave documentation for "the end of the day" — record each action as it happens. Escalation does not apply directly here, though incomplete documentation should itself be treated as a gap to fix immediately. A known limitation: the handbook refers to a "case record" generically and does not name a specific database or software system — this programme does the same, and never invents a system name.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 44 ("Audit Trail & Logging Standards") and Chapter 46 ("Documentation & Record Keeping — The Documentation Standard")'),
  lesson('cs-m7-l3', 'cs-m7', 3, 'Warehouse, Postage and Internal Coordination',
    'Many resolutions cannot be completed by an agent alone — a return needs the Warehouse Contact to confirm receipt and condition, a replacement needs the Warehouse Contact to confirm stock and dispatch, and a label or an urgent dispatch investigation needs the Postage Team Contact. The handbook\'s rule is that all of this internal communication goes through the designated internal channel for that team, never a personal phone call or message, and a return or replacement is never confirmed to the customer as "done" until the relevant internal team has actually confirmed it — a vague internal impression is not the same as a confirmation. Where a genuine product-quality issue is confirmed — a specific SKU fault, or the same SKU generating repeated defect claims — that is reported through the Team Head, who decides whether and how to notify the supplier; this is never something an agent does independently. This matters because promising a customer an outcome before it is actually internally confirmed creates a promise the business cannot necessarily keep. The required check is internal confirmation from the relevant team before any customer-facing confirmation; the required evidence is that internal confirmation itself — dispatch date, tracking number, courier, or return condition. The permitted action is relaying a confirmed status to the customer once internally verified; supplier-notification decisions belong to Team Head. It is never acceptable to confirm a dispatch, return receipt, or replacement to a customer without the internal team\'s own confirmation, or to share warehouse contact details with a customer. Escalate a batch or repeated-SKU quality issue to Team Head.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 47 ("Supplier Communication System") and Chapter 48 ("Warehouse Communication Workflow — Warehouse Communication Rules")'),
  lesson('cs-m7-l4', 'cs-m7', 4, 'OOS, Escalation Framework and Unresolved-Case Handover',
    'An out-of-stock order is handled so it protects both the customer relationship and the marketplace account, and the correct process differs by channel — for example, an eBay out-of-stock order must be marked as dispatched before it is cancelled through the correct process, never cancelled directly with an out-of-stock reason code. Before cancelling any out-of-stock order, the handbook requires checking whether a suitable alternative exists; if so, the customer is offered a choice between the alternative and a full refund, and an alternative is never dispatched without the customer\'s explicit agreement. Every escalation to Team Head must include four elements: a case summary, what has been verified, what has already been told to the customer, and the specific decision needed — a vague "not sure what to do" escalation is not sufficient. Where no documented rule covers a scenario at all, the No-Match Protocol from Module 1 applies in full: a holding message, a complete escalation, and the decision gets fed back so a new rule can be written. This matters because an incomplete escalation just moves the uncertainty to someone else without giving them what they need to decide quickly. The required check is the four escalation elements, every time; the required evidence is whatever has already been captured for the case. The permitted action is escalating with a complete summary. It is never acceptable to dispatch an out-of-stock alternative without the customer\'s explicit agreement, or to escalate without all four required elements. Escalate, always, for any scenario with no matching documented rule.',
    6, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 49 ("Inventory & Out-of-Stock Handling") and Chapter 50 ("Internal Escalation Framework — Escalation Decision Matrix; How to Escalate Correctly")'),

  // ---- Module 8 — Canonical References and Golden Principles ----
  lesson('cs-m8-l1', 'cs-m8', 1, 'Canonical Claim-Window and Category Registers',
    'Several chapters near the end of the handbook are explicitly written as the single authoritative version of a rule that appears in shorter form earlier — Chapter 51 is the canonical source for every claim-window question, superseding the shorter mentions in the returns and warranty chapters, and Chapter 54 is the canonical register of the 11 message categories, superseding the shorter mention in the communication chapter. This matters because when a canonical register exists, relying on an earlier, shorter mention instead risks missing a detail the canonical version adds — the handbook itself states that these later chapters are the authoritative version, not a duplicate to be chosen between. The required check is, before relying on an earlier chapter\'s summary of a rule, confirming whether a later canonical register exists for that same rule and using it instead; there is no separate evidence requirement for this lookup skill itself. The permitted action is citing the canonical chapter when applying claim-window or category rules in a case. It is never acceptable to treat an early, shorter mention as more authoritative than the canonical register that supersedes it. Escalation does not apply to this lesson directly. A known limitation: this lesson does not repeat the full window or category tables already taught in Modules 2 and 4 — it teaches the lookup and precedence skill on top of what those modules already cover.',
    5, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 51 ("Claim Window Definitions — Start Date, Duration & Rules") and Chapter 54 ("Message Category Register — CS-061")'),
  lesson('cs-m8-l2', 'cs-m8', 2, 'Pre-Send Checklist and Evidence Matrix',
    'Chapter 52 is the full, authoritative specification of the nine-point pre-send checklist: data accuracy and order reference verified; tone professional and calm; the action and outcome stated clearly; specific next steps with a named timeframe; platform rules not violated; no internal information disclosed; required approval obtained before sending; the safety protocol completed first if this is a safety case; and, if a document is attached, that it is the correct and unexpired one for this exact order. Each of the nine checks has its own specific failure mode to avoid, and a failure on the safety check specifically means stopping entirely and following the safety protocol before anything is sent. Chapter 55 is the canonical, expanded version of the evidence matrix, adding an explicit Team Head approval column to the evidence requirements already taught in Module 7. This matters because the checklist takes under thirty seconds and catches the specific, repeatable failure modes the source describes, such as sending the wrong order\'s attachment or an unapproved above-threshold commitment. All nine checks are required, every message, without exception; the required evidence is exactly what the evidence matrix already sets out, now with the approval column applied. A message may be sent only once all nine checks pass; check 7 specifically enforces the Module 1 approval thresholds before sending. It is never acceptable to send a message that fails any of the nine checks. Check 8\'s failure mode requires escalation before any message is sent at all.',
    5, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 52 ("Pre-Send Message Checklist — CS-042") and Chapter 55 ("Evidence Requirement Matrix — CS-045")'),
  lesson('cs-m8-l3', 'cs-m8', 3, 'Supported Complete Templates and Template-Use Controls',
    'Chapter 56 is the canonical register of roughly thirty named reusable message templates, each tied to a specific scenario and source chapter, and the rule is that a template is always the approved starting point, always personalised with the customer\'s real details before sending, and its ID is always logged in the case record. Not every named template in that register has its complete wording written out elsewhere in the handbook — a smaller number of templates do have complete wording available, covering scenarios such as a standard holding message, a delivery update while a parcel is moving normally or genuinely delayed, a lost-parcel resolution offer once a courier confirms the loss, a damage-evidence request, a warranty-claim acknowledgement, and a safety-issue response; this programme reproduces only that supported subset, cited by its exact chapter and template label. This matters because assembling a plausible-sounding template from a one-line scenario description risks getting the mandatory wording, placeholders, or prohibited content wrong. The required check is whether a template\'s full wording is actually available before using it as a finished message; there is no separate evidence requirement for this lookup skill. The permitted action is using a complete template as its starting point, personalised for the case, or composing an original, checklist-compliant message where no complete template exists, logged as "ORIGINAL." It is never acceptable to present an invented message as if it were an approved template, or to combine two unrelated examples into one. Where wording is missing, request it from the Content Owner rather than guessing. A known limitation: this programme only teaches templates whose complete source wording was actually found in the handbook; the rest of the Chapter 56 register is known by name and scenario only, never as usable message content.',
    5, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 56 ("Canonical Template Register — CS-070")'),
  lesson('cs-m8-l4', 'cs-m8', 4, 'Ten Golden Principles and Final End-to-End Case Review',
    'Chapter 57 closes the handbook with ten short, memorable principles that summarise everything taught across this programme: never reply without checking the data; always verify tracking before making any delivery statement; protect the marketplace accounts at all times; avoid unnecessary refunds by following the resolution priority order; evidence before action, always; maintain professionalism regardless of the customer\'s tone; protect customer trust by resolving issues correctly, not just quickly; protect the company financially by following approval thresholds; document everything before closing any case; and escalate when uncertain, because escalating is the correct action, not a failure. Applying these to a full case end to end means working through classification and priority, verification, evidence, the correct resolution tier and its approval boundary, a checklist-compliant reply, complete documentation, and — where nothing matches — an escalation, in that order, every time. This matters because these ten principles are designed to be memorised, since they are what an agent falls back on in the rare situation no specific rule quite covers. The required check is running a completed case back through all ten principles as a final self-check; the completed case record itself is the required evidence. A case may be closed only once every principle is satisfied, with the approval boundary being whatever the specific case required from the earlier modules. It is never acceptable to close a case that skips documentation, or that resolves uncertainty by guessing instead of escalating. Escalate whenever any of the ten principles is not clearly satisfied. A known limitation: this lesson is the programme\'s final review step and introduces no new rule beyond what Modules 1 through 7 already taught.',
    5, 'Ledsone Customer Support Handbook, Chapter 57 ("The Ten Golden Principles")'),
];
